package sentinel

// kof.sentinel.http — the HTTP WIRING of the sentinel (universal platform
// Stage 5.9, the wiring half): `sentinelHttp` turns a Sentinel into a web
// middleware for `app.use(...)` — an OBSERVER in the defensive-only law of
// this library: it records one event per request (source = the request
// path, so the visibility is per endpoint; kind = "request") and ALWAYS
// passes through (returns null), never blocking, never mutating the
// response. The burst rule stays the caller's decision (`bursting` /
// `count` over the recorded (source, kind) pairs) — a middleware that
// decided by itself would be policy where the library supplies mechanism.
//
// The event timestamp is the REAL clock (`time.now()`, a Long, matching
// `addEvent`'s atMillis) — inside a live request the middleware is the
// only honest clock holder; a caller needing a synthetic clock drives
// `addEvent` directly instead. The middleware chain runs BEFORE the
// security pipeline and the routes (measured in JvmRuntimeWebDispatch),
// so the observation sees every request the app sees, including ones the
// security pipeline later rejects.
//
// Uses only the measured web ambient accessors (`path()`), the measured
// `time.now()` (Long) and the landed Sentinel — no engine, no FFI, no new
// primitive; the log-ingestion pump face lives in LogPump.kf.

// sentinelHttp — wrap a Sentinel as a pass-through web middleware.
() -> String? sentinelHttp(Sentinel sent) {
    return () -> {
        sent.addEvent(path(), "request", time.now())
        return null
    }
}
